# Binary assets must never go through line-ending conversion.
# core.autocrlf=true on the Windows machines was rewriting LF to CRLF inside
# PDFs on checkout, which changes their byte length and can corrupt a file that
# is later committed back from that working copy. Print files are the ones that
# actually hurt: a mangled PDF still returns 200 and still opens as "a PDF",
# so nothing tells you it broke until it is on a print shop counter.
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.zip   binary

# 2026-09-20 SYS incident: review/archive/ grew to 119MB of retired review
# pages (never pruned), pushing the `git archive` payload the autodeploy
# cron ships to Hostinger's TUS upload past ~180MB. That payload size
# intermittently exceeds the upload's internal timeout in
# scripts/deploy-static-hostinger.mjs, which then throws mid-retry trying
# to reopen the already-consumed archive stream (ENOENT on the temp zip).
# Net effect: most 5-min deploy ticks failed silently-ish (alerted but easy
# to miss), so brand-new review pages (e.g. 20260920-data-activation-positioning)
# sat correctly on origin/main for hours while the live site 404'd them.
# export-ignore keeps review/archive/ fully tracked in git history/tree
# (nothing deleted, nothing lost) but drops it from `git archive` output,
# cutting the deploy payload from ~180MB to ~60MB. Revert this line if any
# archived review page's direct URL needs to stay live on the real site.
review/archive/** export-ignore
